The '''TIE pilot flight suit'''<ref name="Manual" /> was the [[flight suit]] worn by [[Imperial Starfighter Pilot|TIE fighter pilots]] of the [[Galactic Empire]]. The suits used self-contained [[life support]] systems as [[TIE/ln space superiority starfighter|TIE fighters]] were not pressurized.

==Description==
Most [[pilot]]s of the [[Imperial Navy]] wore the standard [[Color|black]] TIE pilot flight suit, which was pressurized and made of energy-shielded fabric.<ref name="Manual" /><ref name="Encyclopedia">''[[Star Wars: The Visual Encyclopedia]]''</ref> Pilots also wore a reinforced, vacuum-sealed [[helmet]] with polarized lenses and ship-linked displays and communication equipment.<ref name="VG">''[[Star Wars Rebels: The Visual Guide]]''</ref><ref name="Manual" /> Chest [[armor]], an emergency atmospheric unit and a life support pack were worn on top of the suit, while positive-[[gravity]] pressure [[boot]]s and insulated [[gauntlet]]s covered the wearer's feet and hands.<ref name="Manual" /><ref name="RO Visual Guide">''[[Star Wars: Rogue One: The Ultimate Visual Guide]]''</ref>

While Imperial pilot gear was not usually adorned with markings, there were notable exceptions. The elite pilot [[Baron of the Empire|Baron]] [[Valen Rudor]] had red markings on his helmet and flight suit while stripes and other types of markings were awarded to some Imperial pilots.<ref name="Empire Day">{{Rebels|Empire Day (episode)}}</ref><ref name="Squadrons">''[[Star Wars: Squadrons]]''</ref> Some pilots who flew during the [[Mid Rim uprisings]] had dark purple flight suits with gold markings, emblematic of the monarchies they had helped subjugate.<ref name="Squadrons" /> A mysterious group of pilots who wore red flight suits with white markings were rumored to have proven absolute loyalty to the emperor.<ref name="Squadrons" /> Pilots who were part of high command had helmets with red stripes.<ref name="Squadrons" /> Some wealthy elite and aristocratic pilots had helmets plated with semi-precious metals.<ref name="Squadrons" /> Exceptional [[Carida Academy]] cadets could have earned yellow stripes on their gear.<ref name="Squadrons" /> Similarly, elite Imperial pilots like [[Captain]] [[Vult Skerris]] who taught at the [[Skystrike Academy|Skystrike]] or Carida academies used yellow markings.<ref name="Squadrons" /><ref name="Empire Day">{{Rebels|Empire Day (episode)}}</ref> Trainees at Skystrike Academy wore the academy's emblem on their helmets in addition to the [[Imperial crest]].<ref name="The Antilles Extraction">{{Rebels|The Antilles Extraction}}</ref> Pilots who were lucky enough to survive being shot down simply wore the burned and pitted markings on their helmets as a symbol of their service to the empire.<ref name="Squadrons" />

===Variant forms===
====Specialized flight suits====
[[File:SoloCadetDeletedScene.png|thumb|right|200px|[[Han Solo]] in a cadet flight suit and helmet]]
The standard black flight suit of Imperial Navy pilots was supplemented by various other suits and helmets that could be granted to exceptional pilots or assigned for specialized roles. 

Both the most ruthless instructors and most skilled cadets could be granted armor with special yellow markings indicating their affiliation with an imperial naval academy. Some imperial naval cadets wore [[Imperial cadet flight suit|open-faced helmets]] similar to those worn by clone [[Aggressive ReConnaissance-170 starfighter|ARC-170]] pilots and used a different chest box.<ref name="Squadrons" /><ref name="Solo">''[[Solo: A Star Wars Story]]''</ref> Carida Academy trainees who participated in advanced reconnaissance training wore a similar black, white and blue helmet, while pilots flying with the [[224th Imperial Armored Division|224th Armored Division]] of the [[Imperial Army]] wore a grey and black version and could be referred to as a “trench jockey.”<ref name="Squadrons" />

A [[Reinforced flight suit|reinforced version]] of the standard flight suit was used by pilots during particularly brutal or dangerous conditions. This armor incorporated additional armor on the arms and legs as well as a unique reinforced life support unit on the chest.

TIE pilots with exceptional survival records may have been permitted to wear a [[technical flight suit]] and helmet. This flight suit was grey, used a different helmet, gloves, boots and chest box, and had additional equipment on the chest. These pilots also had access to additional survival and repairs supplies not afforded to typical pilots.<ref name="Squadrons" />

Tie pilots encountering particularly hazardous conditions may be issued a [[hazard flight suit]]. This suit featured heavier, insulated materials and equipment to ward off a variety of hazards encountered both in space and planetside. Pilots operating at maritime bases were known to wear a variant of this armor that matched the color of the [[Coastal defender stormtrooper|shoretroopers]] they served alongside.

==Behind the scenes==
TIE pilot flight suits first appeared in ''[[Star Wars: Episode IV A New Hope]]''.<ref name="ANH">{{Film|IV}}</ref>

